        "verbatim": "The most well-known acetaldehyde dehydrogenase inhibitors are:\n\n1. Disulfiram (Antabuse)\n2. Calcium carbimide (Temposil)\n3. Cyanamide (Calcium cyanamide)\n4. Metronidazole (Flagyl)\n5. Captopril (Capoten)\n6. Quercetin (a flavonoid found in some fruits and vegetables)\n7. Chloral hydrate\n8. Acriflavine (an antiseptic dye)\n\nThese compounds prevent the metabolism of acetaldehyde to acetic acid, leading to an accumulation of acetaldehyde in the body, which causes unpleasant symptoms such as facial flushing, nausea, headache, and vomiting if alcohol is consumed.",
